---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro"
import Text from "@components/core/Text.astro"
import { Pages } from "@utils"
import PartnerFooter from "@components/PartnerFooter.astro"
---

<Layout title="Tennibot vs. Volley | Tennibot" showFooter={false} showHeader={false} showPartnerHeader={true} showSecondaryHeader={false} showSecondaryMenu={false}>
    <main class="bg-t-off-black overflow-hidden text-t-off-white" >
        <section class="w-[80%] mx-auto mt-36 mb-24" >
            <Text tag="h1" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-center md:text-4xl text-2xl mb-10" >Tennibot Partner vs. Volley: What You Need to Know</Text>
        </section>

        <section class="w-[80%] mx-auto" >
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                We often get asked about other ball-collecting devices like Volley and how they compare to Tennibot’s Partner. Here’s a no-fluff breakdown to help you make an informed choice if you're serious about automating your training.
            </Text>

            <Text tag="h3" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-xl mb-10" >
                1. Price Tag vs. Value
            </Text>
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                Let’s start with the obvious: <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >Volley costs between $1,500 and $3,000 per month</span>. That’s up to <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >$36,000 per year</span>, with no ownership. It’s a rental-only model targeted at clubs, not individuals. Even if you're just exploring new ways to train, that's a steep experiment.
                <br /><br /><span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >Tennibot Partner?</span> It’s yours for a <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >one-time price of less than $3K</span>—less than a single month of Volley—and it comes with a 60-day risk-free return and a 3-year warranty. That's it!
            </Text>

            <Text tag="h3" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-xl mb-10" >
                2. Both Use AI—but One Actually Learns
            </Text>
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                Volley uses AI to track your position and fire balls based on presets. But it’s still largely pre-scripted—what you set is what you get.
                <br /><br />Tennibot Partner is different. With <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >real-time adaptive learning, Match My Level mode</span>, and <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >three 4K synchronized cameras</span>, the Partner doesn’t just follow commands—it responds. It evolves with your game, pushing you just enough to keep you improving without overwhelming you.
            </Text>

            <Text tag="h3" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-xl mb-10" >
                3. Stationary vs. Moving
            </Text>
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                Volley’s machine stays put. That’s fine if you want consistent reps from one location.
                <br /><br />But tennis isn’t static, and neither is Tennibot Partner. It moves <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >autonomously across the court</span>, shifting angles and positions to replicate live match play. It brings randomness and realism into your training, helping you react, move, and hit like you’re actually in a rally.
            </Text>

            <Text tag="h3" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-xl mb-10" >
                4. Portability 
            </Text>
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                Volley's main selling point is its ability to shoot serves, which is definitely cool! However, this cool feature comes at the expense of its portability and mobility. It is heavy and relatively large, making it more suitable for a club than an individual.
            </Text>

            <Text tag="h3" className="font-avenirBold text-t-green text-xl mb-10" >
                Final Thoughts
            </Text>
            <Text tag="p" className="font-plutoLight text-t-off-white text-base mb-10" >
                If you just want to practice return of serve, and money/portability is no object, then <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >Volley</span> is a good choice. If you want to play with a human-like ball machine at a competitive price with tons of AI capabilities and peace of mind of US-based support, then <span class="font-avenirBold text-t-green" >Tennibot</span> is the right choice.
            </Text>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
<PartnerFooter />